From ea4f9dc74e54a0b85241c529f446f17b49b774aa Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 11:35:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] fix(update-pause): a notifier wake claimed before
 the hold re-queues instead of starting inside it

laptop-1 incident 31763 (hermes-sync 31763-1794567981543, 2026-11-13 PST, core fedc433cef): the restart-safe
drain lost its 180 s claim lease to the Desktop chat 20261026_235722_bef6c6.

Timeline (agent.log/gateway.log/gui.log, state.db session_admissions, read-only):
- 02:24:06 the legacy Desktop backend (tui_gateway, pid 63130) starts a turn on bef6c6 that holds the
  cross-process session turn lease for 2586 s.
- 02:54:34 the canonical gateway (pid 63148) claims the next FIFO row of the same chat, admission
  fba3e1457ef2441db0a6e03080e204c4 (seq 4233): the folded Kanban-wake batch ("28 Kanban task updates ...",
  identity ["kanban-wake", route, ...] = request_id, so session_notifier_drain.notifier_wake is True).
  No hold exists yet, so the claim's notifier-hold check passes. Its agent turn then blocks in
  turn_facade.admit_durable_turn_lease behind the Desktop turn.
- 03:06:22 the drain takes its claim hold; 03:07:04 it asks the 4 live turns to pause.
- 03:07:12.09 the Desktop turn parks (paused_for_update) and releases the lease. 12.278 the legacy backend's own
  queued-prompt drain is correctly refused (HOLD_MESSAGE). 12.353 the gateway turn that had been waiting on
  the lease acquires it and STARTS (platform=desktop, history=105) inside the window.
- 03:07:21 that turn's turn-start preflight compression runs (deep aux model): no step boundary for ~2 min.
- 03:09:04 reclaim preflight refuses ("unmatched gateway work"); 03:09:07 the wake parks; 03:09:22 lease lost.

Five whys:
1. Why did a new turn start in the window? A wake admission claimed at 02:54 began executing at 03:07:12.
2. Why did it begin then? Its turn waited on the session turn lease, and the lease freed the moment the
   Desktop turn parked for the update.
3. Why was the hold not checked? The claim checks the hold class (session_notifier_drain.eligible) once, at
   claim time; nothing re-checked it when the claimed turn actually began.
4. Why could the turn not just pause? A turn pauses only at a completed-tool step boundary (gateway checkpoints
   need a persisted tool suffix); a turn that has not started has none, and its turn-start compression cannot
   yield (compression_can_yield excludes turn-start preflight: there is no boundary to land on).
5. Root cause: claim admission and turn start are separated by an unbounded wait, and the hold contract
   ("new turns wait in their backend's queue") was enforced only at the first.
It was NOT session_auto_continue._drain_queued_prompt (that drain was refused at 12.278) and not a queued claim
during the hold (_claim_next skips held notifier wakes).

Fix:
- agent/update_pause_cancel.py: pause_before_turn (already run after the lease is acquired and before the turn
  body, i.e. before the turn-start compression) also asks a per-run gate, vars(agent)["update_yield_before_turn"].
  A truthy reason ends the turn unstarted (no request, nothing persisted, transcript unchanged) and the result
  carries update_pause_unstarted=True.
- gateway/session_update_pause_ingress.py + session_update_pause_hook.py: for a fresh claim of the claim-held
  class (a notifier wake, not a resumed checkpoint) the bound scope carries the claim's own hold test
  (session_notifier_drain.held_noted); the installed hook sets the gate for that run and removes it after. A
  user Stop or steer pending wins. Humans and checkpoint resumes are untouched (they keep their admission
  semantics under the hold).
- gateway/run_turn_runner.py: project_paused_result passes the unstarted marker through.
- hermes_state_update_pause.requeue_unstarted_input: guarded started -> queued for the exact owner epoch and
  generation; refused once any transcript row of the conversation's chain postdates the claim's transcript_mark,
  or a worker, steering transfer or checkpoint exists. seq is untouched (the row is the FIFO head again) and the
  old generation is fenced (its settle/checkpoint refuse).
- gateway/session_update_pause.park_or_interrupt: an unstarted pause re-queues (waiters and media stay with the
  row, execution stamp cleared, pending accounting republished); the drain then sees notifier_held and waits
  until the hold lifts. A refused re-queue falls back to the existing checkpoint path.

Compression decision: compression stays NOT a pause point. Turn-start/idle/engine preflight compressions have no
step boundary to park on (a gateway checkpoint needs a completed tool suffix; the Desktop side refuses them in
compression_can_yield for the same reason, review 5878929471 on #1617), and mid-turn post-tool/pre-API
compressions are already cancellable through the update cancel. The bug was entering a turn whose pause predated
it; the turn-start gate now runs before that compression, so such a turn never reaches it.

Tests:
- tests/gateway/test_update_hold_claimed_wake.py: composed GatewayRunner + SessionAuthority + real
  admit_route_wake: a wake claimed before the hold, whose turn begins after it, goes back to queued (unsettled,
  unrun, not parked, execution cleared), stays queued across claim polls while held, then runs once as the same
  admission and completes after the lift. RED on origin/main ("the wake began its turn inside the update hold").
  Plus: a human row gets no turn-start gate.
- tests/agent/test_update_yield_before_turn.py: real AIAgent: the gate yields before any model request,
  persistence or turn-start compression (_compress_context never called); open and failing gates run normally.
- tests/hermes_state/test_requeue_unstarted_input.py: FIFO head again, old generation fenced, re-claim bumps the
  generation; refused after a transcript write, for another generation/epoch, and after settlement.
